Title: http package - net/http - Go Packages

URL Source: https://pkg.go.dev/net/http

Markdown Content:
Package http provides HTTP client and server implementations.

[Get](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Get), [Head](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Head), [Post](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Post), and [PostForm](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#PostForm) make HTTP (or HTTPS) requests:

resp, err := http.Get("http://example.com/")
resp, err := http.Post("http://example.com/upload", "image/jpeg", &buf)
resp, err := http.PostForm("http://example.com/form",
url.Values{"key": {"Value"}, "id": {"123"}})

The caller must close the response body when finished with it:

if err != nil {
// handle error
defer resp.Body.Close()
body, err := io.ReadAll(resp.Body)

#### Clients and Transports [¶](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#hdr-Clients_and_Transports "Go to Clients and Transports")

For control over HTTP client headers, redirect policy, and other settings, create a [Client](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Client):

client := &http.Client{
CheckRedirect: redirectPolicyFunc,

resp, err := client.Get("http://example.com")

req, err := http.NewRequest("GET", "http://example.com", nil)
req.Header.Add("If-None-Match", `W/"wyzzy"`)
resp, err := client.Do(req)

For control over proxies, TLS configuration, keep-alives, compression, and other settings, create a [Transport](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Transport):

tr := &http.Transport{
MaxIdleConns:       10,
IdleConnTimeout:    30 * time.Second,
DisableCompression: true,
client := &http.Client{Transport: tr}
resp, err := client.Get("https://example.com")

Clients and Transports are safe for concurrent use by multiple goroutines and for efficiency should only be created once and re-used.

#### Servers [¶](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#hdr-Servers "Go to Servers")

ListenAndServe starts an HTTP server with a given address and handler. The handler is usually nil, which means to use [DefaultServeMux](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#DefaultServeMux). [Handle](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Handle) and [HandleFunc](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#HandleFunc) add handlers to [DefaultServeMux](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#DefaultServeMux):

http.Handle("/foo", fooHandler)

http.HandleFunc("/bar", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
fmt.Fprintf(w, "Hello, %q", html.EscapeString(r.URL.Path))

log.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe(":8080", nil))

More control over the server's behavior is available by creating a custom Server:

s := &http.Server{
Addr:           ":8080",
Handler:        myHandler,
ReadTimeout:    10 * time.Second,
WriteTimeout:   10 * time.Second,
MaxHeaderBytes: 1 << 20,
log.Fatal(s.ListenAndServe())

#### HTTP/2 [¶](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#hdr-HTTP_2 "Go to HTTP/2")

The http package has transparent support for the HTTP/2 protocol.

[Server](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Server) and [DefaultTransport](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#DefaultTransport) automatically enable HTTP/2 support when using HTTPS. [Transport](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Transport) does not enable HTTP/2 by default.

To enable or disable support for HTTP/1, HTTP/2, and/or unencrypted HTTP/2, see the [Server.Protocols](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Server.Protocols) and [Transport.Protocols](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Transport.Protocols) configuration fields.

To configure advanced HTTP/2 features, see the [Server.HTTP2](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Server.HTTP2) and [Transport.HTTP2](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Transport.HTTP2) configuration fields.

Alternatively, the following GODEBUG settings are currently supported:

GODEBUG=http2client=0  # disable HTTP/2 client support
GODEBUG=http2server=0  # disable HTTP/2 server support
GODEBUG=http2debug=1   # enable verbose HTTP/2 debug logs
GODEBUG=http2debug=2   # ... even more verbose, with frame dumps

The "omithttp2" build tag may be used to disable the HTTP/2 implementation contained in the http package.

